\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A noncentral involution with scalar represented action}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{On #{M=\mathbb R}, let #{A=\mathbb R\times M_2(\mathbb R)}
act by #{\alpha(a,B)=a\bbI}. Use the standard basis map
#{(x,y)\mapsto xe_1+ye_2} on the second factor's module and put
#{\omega=(1,\operatorname{diag}(1,-1))}. Then #{\omega^2=1}
and #{T=\alpha(\omega)=\bbI}. For the matrix unit
#{E_{12}=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\0&0\end{pmatrix}},
##{\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)E_{12}=E_{12},\qquad
E_{12}\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)=-E_{12}.}
Thus #{\omega} is not central in #{A}, although the projectors of
its represented involution are #{\bbI} and zero and are #{A}-linear.}

\p{This calculation supplies the strict distinction in
\ref{fdip-003O}. The representation and group-algebra conventions
are \citet{Sections 3.1-3.2, pp. 39-41}{sengupta2010representations}.}
